---
// Gizlilik politikası. Gerçek davranışa dayanır: sunucu (server/README.md, server/src/account.ts, cleanup.ts,
// transcribe.ts, deepgram.ts, llm/, auth/, billing.ts, referrals.ts, trialLedger.ts, deviceCheck.ts, telemetry.ts, funnel.ts, migrations/, wrangler.jsonc), abonelik uygulaması
// (App/Hosted: canlı metin Deepgram'la, kapasite dolunca ya da bağlantı yoksa Mac'te; sesle yazma tamamen Mac'te, Erişilebilirlik
// yalnızca ⌘V için (App/Dictation.swift); paylaşılan ekranın görüntüleri isteğe bağlı, Mac'te kalır, yalnızca yerel MCP
// istemcisine verilir (App/ScreenSnapshots.swift, AINotepadCore/Screen, MCP/MCPScreens.swift); ayrı bir ayarla en çok 6'sı
// küçültülüp not isteğiyle sunucu üzerinden Anthropic'e gider, sunucu saklamaz ve loglamaz (AINotepadCore/Screen/ScreenNotes.swift,
// server/src/llm/images.ts); iCloud eşitlemesi yok, güncellemeler
// GitHub'dan; sürüm başlıkları, yapay zekâ isteğinin amacı ve Ayarlar › Genel'den kapatılabilen kullanım
// istatistikleri; takvim EventKit'le cihazda okunur, toplantı bağlı etkinliğin başlığını ve katılımcı adlarını alır: adlar
// Deepgram/ElevenLabs'e ipucu, Anthropic'e konuşmacı adlandırma için gider (Meeting/CalendarMeetings.swift `keytermsWithAttendees`,
// SpeakerReview.swift), brifing etkinliğin başlığını ve en çok 8 katılımcıyı Sor'a sorar (`briefingQuestion`); içe aktarılan ses
// ve video dosyası toplantı gibi işlenir), iPhone uygulaması (iOS/: aynı hesap ve sunucu; yüz yüze toplantı mikrofonla, yedek canlı metin Apple'ın
// cihaz içi konuşma tanımasıyla, App Attest yok, toplantılar telefonda, kullanım istatistikleri Ayarlar › Gizlilik'te) ve bu sitenin tarayıcı depolaması (src/client/theme.js,
// src/client/session.ts). Sağlayıcıların saklama ve eğitim kuralları resmî belgelerinden alınmıştır (2026-10-29):
// ElevenLabs (elevenlabs.io/dpa, docs "Zero Retention Mode" ve "Data residency", terms-of-use-eu §4), Anthropic
// (anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms ve /legal/data-processing-addendum, privacy.claude.com saklama makalesi,
// platform.claude.com "Data residency"), Link (link.com/terms "Sold Through Link Terms", link.com/privacy), Deepgram
// (developers.deepgram.com "Your Data at Deepgram" ve "Model Improvement Partnership Program": `mip_opt_out=true` ile ses
// ve metin yalnızca istek işlenirken tutulur, model geliştirmede kullanılmaz; kullanım kayıtları içerik içermez, 90 gün).
// App Store abonelikleri (iPhone, server/src/appStore.ts): RevenueCat işleyendir (revenuecat.com/privacy "Data
// Processors", revenuecat.com/dpa; ABD'de AWS); Apple App Store satışında bağımsız sorumludur. Sunucu RevenueCat'ten
// yalnızca aboneliğin ürününü, durumunu, dönemini ve sandbox işaretini okur (göç 0048). Nihai metin önbelleği
// (server/src/transcriptionCache.ts) 2 saat sonra sunulmaz, satırı günlük temizlik (cleanup.ts, cron "17 3 * * *") siler.
// Linear/Jira iş açma ajanı (`task_agent`), notu paylaşım diline çevirme (`shared_note_translation`) ve nottan takip
// e-postası taslağı (`follow_up_email`; alıcı adresleri gönderilmez, yalnızca cihazdaki e-posta uygulamasına gider) sunucu
// üzerinden Anthropic'e gider. Mac'teki cihaz içi konuşma modelleri (WhisperKit, SpeakerKit) Hugging Face'ten (huggingface.co,
// argmaxinc/whisperkit-coreml) indirilir. iPhone uygulaması App Store'dadır. Yapay zekâ asistanı erişimi
// (docs/superpowers/specs/2026-11-05-remote-mcp.md; server/src/assistant/, src/mcp/): uygulamada açılır, varsayılan
// kapalı; notların sunucunun okuyabildiği şifreli kopyası AB D1 + R2'de, bağlantı kayıtları KV'de (AB dışında olabilir);
// kapatınca hemen silinir, erişim bitince 7 gün içinde; jetonlar 1 saat / 30 gün boşta / en çok 180 gün.
import LegalSection from "../components/LegalSection.astro";
import Legal from "../layouts/Legal.astro";
import { cardStatementDescriptor, productName } from "../config";
import { copy } from "../copy";
import { type DataHandlingRow, dataHandling } from "../data-handling";
import type { LegalSectionInfo } from "../lib/legal";
import { operator } from "../legal/operator";
import { routes } from "../routes";

const sections: readonly LegalSectionInfo[] = [
  { id: "controller", title: "Who is responsible" },
  { id: "summary", title: "The short version" },
  { id: "meetings", title: "Your meeting content" },
  { id: "data", title: "Data we process, why, and on what legal basis" },
  { id: "messages", title: "Contact form, email, feedback and crash reports" },
  { id: "statistics", title: "App version and usage statistics" },
  { id: "website", title: "This website, cookies and browser storage" },
  { id: "providers", title: "Service providers and international transfers" },
  { id: "retention", title: "How long we keep data" },
  { id: "deletion", title: "Deleting your account" },
  { id: "rights", title: "Your rights" },
  { id: "security", title: "Security" },
  { id: "decisions", title: "Automated decisions" },
  { id: "children", title: "Children" },
  { id: "changes", title: "Changes to this policy" },
  { id: "contact", title: "Contact and company details" },
];
const mail: string = `mailto:${operator.email}`;
/** Ödeme tarafının (Stripe, Link, App Store, RevenueCat) tüketiciye açık metinleri. */
const stripePrivacyUrl: string = "https://stripe.com/privacy";
const applePrivacyUrl: string = "https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/";
const revenueCatPrivacyUrl: string = "https://www.revenuecat.com/privacy";
const revenueCatDpaUrl: string = "https://www.revenuecat.com/dpa";
const linkPrivacyUrl: string = "https://link.com/privacy";
const linkTermsUrl: string = "https://link.com/terms#sold-through-link-terms";
---

<Legal page="privacy" meta={copy.privacyMeta} title={copy.common.nav.privacy} sections={sections}>
  <p class="legal-lead">
    This policy explains what personal data is processed when you use {productName} — the Mac app, the iPhone app, the service behind them and this website — why it is processed, who it is shared with, how long it is kept and how you can use your
    rights under the EU General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR).
  </p>

  {/* Özet tablo (data-handling.ts): numaralı bölümlerin dışında, çapaları kalıcı (`#at-a-glance`, `#data-<id>`). */}
  <section class="glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="at-a-glance-title">
    <h2 id="at-a-glance-title">{dataHandling.title}</h2>
    <p>{dataHandling.lead}</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="stack-table table-glance">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">{dataHandling.columns.data}</th>
            <th scope="col">{dataHandling.columns.where}</th>
            <th scope="col">{dataHandling.columns.stored}</th>
            <th scope="col">{dataHandling.columns.retention}</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            dataHandling.rows.map((row: DataHandlingRow) => (
              <tr id={`data-${row.id}`}>
                <td>
                  <strong>{row.data}</strong>{" "}
                  <a class="glance__more" href={`#${row.policySection}`}>
                    {dataHandling.details}
                  </a>
                </td>
                <td>{row.where}</td>
                <td>{row.stored}</td>
                <td>{row.retention}</td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="controller">
    <p>
      {productName} is operated by <strong>{operator.name}</strong>, {operator.legalForm}, business ID (Y-tunnus) {operator.businessId},{" "}
      {operator.address} (“we”, “us”). We are the controller for the personal data described in this policy, with one
      exception: for the personal data of other people in the meetings you record (their voices, what they say, their
      names), you are the controller and we process that data on your behalf as your processor (see section 3 and the{" "}
      <a href={`${routes.terms}#dpa`}>data processing terms</a>). For any privacy question or request, write to{" "}
      <a href={mail}>{operator.email}</a>.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="summary">
    <ul>
      <li>
        Your notes, transcripts and recordings are stored on your Mac or iPhone, in the app that recorded them. Our servers keep no audio, and no notes or
        translations we can read. There are four exceptions: the final transcript, held encrypted so it can be re-sent for 2 hours if the
        connection drops, then deleted at the next daily cleanup (within about a day); only if you turn on sync
        between Mac and iPhone, an end-to-end encrypted copy of your meetings' text that only your devices can open;
        only if you share a note with a link, an end-to-end encrypted copy of that note that only people with the link can
        open; and, only if you turn on AI assistant access (off by default), an encrypted copy of your meeting notes, never
        audio or transcripts, kept in the EU so the AI assistants you connect can read them. That copy is not end-to-end
        encrypted: our servers can read it (see section 3).
      </li>
      <li>
        Meeting audio and text go only to our AI providers to be transcribed and summarised: Deepgram for the live
        transcript (directly from your device), ElevenLabs for the final transcript and Anthropic for notes (both through our
        server, which keeps no audio, keeps no notes unless you turn on AI assistant access, and holds the final transcript
        encrypted for 2 hours for re-sending and deletes it within about a day). Shared-screen
        snapshots go to Anthropic with the note (and a few with your Ask questions) only if you turn on “Use screen
        snapshots in notes”. Deepgram keeps no
        audio or text after transcribing; the others may keep request data for
        a time under their own retention rules (see section 8). We keep only usage numbers (how long, how many tokens) to
        apply the plan limits.
      </li>
      <li>
        We set no cookies of our own and use no advertising trackers on this website. We count visits with Cloudflare Web
        Analytics, which uses no cookies and collects no personal data. Apple and Google sign-in and Cloudflare Turnstile
        may use their own cookies on the pages where you use them (see section 7). We don't sell your data.
      </li>
      <li>
        The apps tell our server their version and the macOS or iOS version, and which feature each AI request is for. Unless
        you turn off “Share anonymous usage statistics” in its settings, it also sends daily reliability and
        feature-usage counts, and reports its first launch and the steps of signing in. None of this contains meeting
        content (see section 6).
      </li>
      <li>
        You can delete your account yourself at any time; the data on our server is deleted immediately, except a few
        records kept for a limited time to prevent abuse and handle payments, such as a pseudonymous record of your
        free-trial use (section 10 lists them all).
      </li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="meetings">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Live transcript.</strong> While you record, the app streams the meeting audio directly from your Mac or iPhone to
        Deepgram, which returns the live transcript. Our server only issues a short-lived access key for each connection
        and records how long the live transcript may run; it never receives this audio. The app asks Deepgram not to keep
        the audio or transcript and not to use them to improve its models. When the capacity we set aside for this is used
        up, when Deepgram can't be reached, or when you're offline, the live transcript is produced on your device by an
        on-device speech model instead (on iPhone, Apple's on-device speech recognition), and that audio does not leave
        your device. The Mac app downloads this model (about 630 MB) from Hugging Face the first time it needs it; Hugging
        Face sees your IP address and app information, as with any download (see section 8). The iPhone app records in-person meetings through the microphone; it cannot capture audio
        from calls in other apps.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Final transcript and speaker separation.</strong> When a meeting ends, or when you import an audio or
        video file, the app sends the recording through our server to ElevenLabs, which returns the transcript. Our server streams the audio through without
        writing it to storage and records its duration. It keeps the returned transcript encrypted for 2 hours, so
        the app can fetch it again if the reply is lost on the way; after that it can no longer be fetched and is deleted at
        the next daily cleanup (within about a day), or immediately when you delete your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notes, live translation, “What did I miss?” and Ask.</strong> The relevant parts of the transcript (and
        of earlier notes, when you use Ask), together with the participant names and terms you have added, are sent
        through our server to Anthropic's Claude model, and the answer returns to your device. The same applies when you
        create Linear or Jira issues from your tasks (the tasks, the meeting title and Linear's or Jira's replies are sent),
        when you translate a note to send it in another language, and when you draft a follow-up email from a note (the
        note, the meeting title and date, the attendee names of a linked calendar event and your name are sent). The optional
        profile fields in Settings (job title, company and company description) are stored on your device and sent with the
        meeting text when a note, an Ask answer or a follow-up email is written, so Claude understands your context. Our
        server does not store or log this text; it records only the number of tokens used.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Web search in Ask (on by default).</strong> When a question on the Ask page needs information that isn't in
        your meetings (for example current facts, definitions, products or prices), Claude may search the web up to 3 times
        for that answer. The search query, which Claude writes from your question and which may contain words from it or
        from the meeting passages Ask read, is sent through our server to Anthropic, which runs the search with its web
        search provider and gives the results back to Claude. Only the query goes to the search provider, not your meetings.
        Our server does not store or log the query or the results; it counts the searches (at most 100 per account a
        month). The answer lists the web pages it used, and you open them in your browser yourself. You can turn this off
        with “Allow web search in Ask” (Mac: Settings › General; iPhone: Settings › Ask); with it off, Ask never searches
        the web. The Mac app with your own Anthropic key offers the same setting and sends the query straight to Anthropic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Screen snapshots and photos in notes (off by default).</strong> If you turn on “Use screen snapshots in
        notes” (Mac: Settings › General) or “Use photos in notes” (iPhone: Settings › Note, or the meeting's Screens tab),
        then when a note is written the app picks up to 6 of the meeting's shared-screen snapshots and the photos you added
        to it (the ones talked about most, leaving out any you unticked on the meeting's Screens tab), downscales them
        to about 800 pixels and sends them with the note request through our server to Anthropic's Claude model, so the note
        can refer to what was on screen. Our server checks their number and size and passes them on; it does not store or
        log the images, and records only the number of tokens used, as for the text. Anthropic handles them on the same
        terms as the transcript (see section 8). With the same setting on, when you ask a question on the Ask page, up to 3
        snapshots that were on screen while the transcript passages Ask read were spoken (again leaving out the unticked
        ones) are downscaled the same way and sent with the question; the answer says how many snapshots it read. With the
        setting off, snapshots and photos are never sent.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Photos you add to a meeting.</strong> You can add a photo of a whiteboard, a slide or a sketch to a meeting:
        on the iPhone with the camera (the document scanner) or from Photos, on the Mac from a file or with Continuity
        Camera. The app stores it with the meeting in its folder on your device, scaled down and re-encoded, which drops the
        original's metadata such as its location. Photos are not synced between your devices and are not sent anywhere
        unless the setting above is on; an AI app you connect through the app's MCP connection can read them like screen
        snapshots. Deleting a photo on the meeting's Screens tab, or deleting the meeting, deletes it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Spotlight, Siri and Shortcuts (on by default).</strong> The app adds each meeting's title, date, attendee and
        speaker names, the note's summary and its tasks to the system search index on your Mac or iPhone, so Spotlight finds
        them and Siri and Shortcuts can ask your meetings, list your open tasks or open a meeting. The transcript, audio,
        screen snapshots, photos and your own notes are never indexed, nor is the sample meeting. The index stays on the
        device; we don't receive it. Turn it off with “Show meetings in Spotlight” (Mac: Settings › General; iPhone:
        Settings); turning it off, signing out or deleting your account removes the index. A question you ask through Siri
        or Shortcuts goes the same way as one typed on the Ask page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Calendar.</strong> If you allow calendar access, the app reads your calendar events on your device to show
        what's next, start recording when a meeting begins (if you turn that on) and link a recording to its event. Your
        calendar is not sent to us. When a recording is linked to an event, the meeting takes the event's title and its
        attendees' names: the names are sent with the audio to Deepgram and ElevenLabs as spelling hints and with the
        transcript to Anthropic, so speakers can be named correctly, and the title is sent with your meeting's notes when
        you use Ask. If you ask for a briefing before a meeting, the event's title and up to 8 attendee names are sent
        with that question to Anthropic.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Storage.</strong> Notes, transcripts, recordings, screen snapshots and the people and terms you add are
        stored in the app's folder on your Mac or iPhone. Unless you turn on sync or AI assistant access (below), the apps do not copy them to any cloud service, and meetings
        recorded on one device do not appear on the other. You can export or delete them in
        the app at any time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sync between Mac and iPhone (optional, off by default).</strong> If you turn on “Sync between Mac and
        iPhone” in the app's Settings, your meetings' text (transcripts, translations, notes and their earlier versions,
        tasks and decisions, titles, folders, your own notes and speaker names), the people you save, your Ask chats, your
        saved folders and Ask recipes, and the settings that follow your account appear in the Gistsmith app on your other
        Mac or iPhone signed in to the same account. Before anything leaves your device, the app encrypts it end to end
        (AES-256-GCM) with a key made on your device that we never receive; we store only that encrypted copy and cannot
        read it. Your other device gets the key from a 32-character link code shown in the app's Settings; our server keeps
        the key only sealed with that code, which we don't have, so keep the code safe: if you lose every device and the
        code, the synced copy can't be opened by anyone (your meetings on your devices are not affected). Audio, screen
        snapshots and voice profiles are not synced; they stay on the device that recorded the meeting. The encrypted copy
        is stored in Cloudflare R2 storage in the EU jurisdiction. Our database keeps only what is needed to run sync: a
        random identifier, the type (meeting, Ask chat, people, settings) and size of each encrypted item, when it changed
        and a random identifier of the device that changed it. Turning sync off in either app deletes the copy on our
        server straight away, for all your devices; your meetings stay on each device. Sync is not a backup: a meeting
        deleted on one device is deleted on the others too (it goes to Recently Deleted there).
      </li>
      <li id="shared-links">
        <strong>Sharing a note with a link (optional).</strong> If you choose “Share with Link” on a meeting, the app
        puts that note (title, date, summary, topics, decisions, open questions, tasks and glossary, and the transcript
        only if you tick “Include transcript”; never audio or screen snapshots) into one file and encrypts it end to end on
        your device (AES-256-GCM) with a new random key made for that link alone. We receive only the encrypted file, its
        size, a random link identifier, the time it was created and when it expires, and which account it belongs to; we
        don't learn which meeting it came from. The key is only in the part of the link after the # sign, which browsers
        never send to any server, so we cannot read a shared note. Anyone you give the link to can read the note in their
        browser on our website (gistsmith.com/s), which decrypts it there; whoever has the link can also pass it on or save
        a copy, so share it only with people you trust. The page loads no analytics or other third-party script. The
        encrypted note is stored in our database in the EU. The app on the device that created a link remembers it, so
        you can copy it again there. You choose how long a link works (7, 30 or 90 days); you can remove it at any time,
        and deleting the meeting in the app removes its links too. A removed or expired link stops working at once.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AI assistant access (optional, off by default).</strong> Off by default. Turn it on and an encrypted copy of
        your notes, never audio, is kept in the EU so the assistants you connect can read them. Turn it off and it is
        deleted at once (backups within 30 days). You turn it on in the Mac or iPhone app under Settings › Connections ›
        AI assistant access; it can't be turned on from the web. While it is on, each of your devices uploads, for each of
        its meetings, the title, date, length, folder and attendee names, the AI note (summary, decisions, open questions,
        tasks with their owners, due dates and whether they are done, outline and glossary) and your own typed notes. It
        never uploads transcripts, audio, screen snapshots or voice profiles, and never the sample meeting from the app's
        introduction. The copy is the meetings of all your devices together; in the app you can remove one device's
        meetings from it. Each account can keep up to 5,000 meetings and 256 MB in the copy. The copy is encrypted at rest
        (AES-256-GCM, with a key for your account) and stored in Cloudflare D1 and R2 in the EU jurisdiction.{" "}
        <strong>It is not end-to-end encrypted: our servers can read it</strong>, which is how our connector at
        mcp.gistsmith.com answers your assistant's searches and requests. You connect an assistant (for example Claude or
        ChatGPT) from the assistant itself: you sign in with Apple or Google on mcp.gistsmith.com and approve read access;
        your devices then show a notice that a new assistant was connected, and the app lists every connection so you can
        disconnect it. A connected assistant can only search, list and read your notes; it can't change or delete
        anything. For each connection we keep a connection record: the assistant's client identifier, its sign-in return
        address, whether it is a verified product, when it was connected and last used, the number of requests, and an
        account identifier, but never any notes. These records are kept in our EU database and in Cloudflare Workers KV,
        which may store them outside the EU. An assistant's access lasts 1 hour at a time and is renewed automatically; a
        connection that isn't used for 30 days, or is 180 days old, has to be approved again. The number of requests is
        limited, and our logs for this feature hold identifiers and counts only, never note text, meeting titles or search
        queries. Turning AI assistant access off deletes the copy straight away (or, if storage can't be reached, at the
        next daily cleanup), disconnects every assistant and revokes its access. If your subscription or trial ends,
        assistants lose access immediately and the copy is deleted within 7 days. Deleting your account deletes it too.
        Please keep in mind: what an assistant reads is handled under that assistant's provider's own terms and privacy
        policy, not ours; and an assistant that also has other tools, such as email or web access, can be steered by text
        in your meetings, so check what it does before you let it act.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Integrations.</strong> When you send a note or tasks to Notion, Slack, Microsoft Teams, Discord, Zoom,
        Linear, Jira, Reminders or Apple Notes, save it as Markdown to a folder you choose (for example an Obsidian
        vault), or send it to a webhook you set up (such as Zapier, Make or n8n), the app sends that content from your Mac
        directly to the destination you chose, using the account or address you connected. It does not pass through our
        server, and the receiving service processes it under its own terms. Two steps first use Anthropic's Claude model
        through our server, as described above: creating Linear or Jira issues, and translating a note you send in another
        language. If you connect an AI assistant on your Mac
        through the app's MCP connection, that assistant can read your meetings (including any screen snapshots) and
        handles them under its own terms.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Voice typing (Mac app).</strong> When you hold or tap the voice typing shortcut, the app records from your
        microphone until you let go or stop it, and transcribes that audio on your Mac: with Apple's on-device dictation on
        macOS 26 or later, and with the on-device speech model downloaded from Hugging Face (see “Live transcript” above) on
        macOS 15 to 25 (voice typing works on every macOS version the app supports). Neither the audio nor the text is sent to us or to any provider,
        and neither is saved; voice typing doesn't open a meeting and doesn't count toward your plan. The app then pastes
        the text into the app you are typing in: it puts the text on the clipboard, sends ⌘V, and restores what was on
        your clipboard. To send ⌘V it needs the macOS Accessibility permission; it uses that permission only for this
        keystroke and does not read your keystrokes, your screen or other apps' content. Without the permission, the text
        is only copied to your clipboard.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shared-screen snapshots (Mac app, off by default).</strong> If you turn on “Take snapshots of the shared
        screen” in Settings › General, then while you record an online meeting the app looks at the screen every few
        seconds and keeps a still image (JPEG) whenever the content changes meaningfully, at most one every 20 seconds and up to 120 per
        meeting; moving video and scrolling are skipped until the screen settles. If it
        finds the call's window (for example Zoom, Teams or a Google Meet tab) it captures only that window; otherwise it
        captures your main display. It never captures the app's own windows, doesn't capture while recording is paused,
        and doesn't capture in in-person mode. This uses the macOS Screen Recording permission, which you grant in System
        Settings. The snapshots are stored with the meeting in the app's folder on your Mac. They are not sent to us,
        to our AI providers or to anyone else, unless you also turn on “Use screen snapshots in notes” (see “Screen
        snapshots in notes” above). An AI app on your Mac can read them only if you connect it through the app's MCP
        connection, and that app's provider then handles what it reads under its own terms. You can delete one snapshot or all of a meeting's snapshots on the meeting's Screens tab; deleting the
        meeting deletes its snapshots. Shared screens can show other people's content, so check that the participants
        agree before you keep snapshots of what they share.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Whose data, and who decides.</strong> For your own voice, your words and the content you add, we process
      meeting content to perform our contract with you. Meetings also involve other people. You decide whether to record a
