  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="messages">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Contact form.</strong> When you write to us through the <a href={routes.contact}>contact form</a>, we
        store your email address and message so that we can answer you. The form is protected by Cloudflare Turnstile
        (see section 7). We delete the message and email address 180 days after we receive them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Email.</strong> If you email us directly, or we answer you by email, the messages are kept in our mailbox,
        hosted by Zoner in Finland, for as long as needed to handle your request and any follow-up.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Feedback and crash reports from the app.</strong> These are sent only when you choose to send them. They
        contain your message or the crash report, the app version and the macOS or iOS version, and — if you are signed in —
        your account ID; an email address is included only if you add one. We use them to answer you and to fix problems,
        and delete them 180 days after we receive them.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="statistics">
    <p>
      <strong>Always sent.</strong> These are part of providing the service and can't be turned off:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>App and OS version.</strong> The {productName} Mac and iPhone apps send their version and build number and
        the macOS or iOS version with their requests to our server. We keep the latest values on your account together with the time
        the app last checked in, to know which versions are in use, to support older versions and to plan updates. They
        are deleted with your account.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Feature of each AI request.</strong> Every AI request says which feature it is for (for example live
        translation, meeting notes or Ask). We keep daily token totals per feature, and the number of web searches made for
        Ask answers, which are not linked to any account. To apply the monthly web search limit we also count each
        account's web searches in its monthly usage (no queries).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Usage statistics.</strong> Only if “Share anonymous usage statistics” is on (it is on by default in the{" "}
      {productName} Mac and iPhone apps), the app also sends:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Reliability and performance counts:</strong> live transcription connection drops and reconnects, final
        transcription giving up, note generation failures, recordings reaching their time limit, and how long final
        transcription and note writing took. These are summed per day and app version.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Daily feature usage:</strong> once a day, which features were used that day — whether live translation was
        used and its target language, whether Ask was used, how many times each integration type (for example Slack,
        Notion or Apple Notes) and each export format (for example PDF or Markdown) was used, whether your meeting archive
        was accessed over MCP, whether the live transcript ran on your device, whether voice typing was used, whether
        shared-screen snapshots were kept, and whether a note was written with screen snapshots (never the snapshots
        themselves).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>First launch and sign-in steps:</strong> once, the first time the app is opened, and when you sign in:
        that the sign-in window was opened, closed, failed (and at which stage: the window didn't open, the sign-in
        return was rejected, our server refused the sign-in, or a network error) or completed, with the sign-in provider
        (Apple or Google). Each step is sent with the app version, also before you have an account, and counted per day
        without any identifier. Your IP address is used only to rate-limit these requests and is not stored.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These statistics are stored only as daily totals and are not linked to your account. To avoid counting the same day
      twice, your account keeps the date of the last daily report and how many statistics uploads were made today.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Never included:</strong> audio, transcripts, notes, prompts, meeting titles, names, email addresses, file
      names or paths, links, or any other meeting content.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Purpose and legal basis.</strong> We use this to find and fix reliability problems, measure performance,
      decide which features to improve and plan capacity and cost. Our legal basis is our legitimate interest in operating
      and improving the service (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)); you can object at any time by turning the setting off.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Retention.</strong> Daily totals are kept for 400 days; the version fields on your account until the
      account is deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How to turn it off.</strong> In the {productName} Mac app, open Settings › General; in the iPhone app, open
      Settings › Privacy. Turn off “Share anonymous usage statistics”. When it is off, the app sends no usage statistics;
      the app and OS version and the feature of each AI request are still sent because the service needs them. The
      self-hosted developer edition (AI-Notepad) sends none of this.
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

  <LegalSection sections={sections} id="website">
    <p>
      This website sets no cookies of its own and uses no advertising or tracking tools. Fonts are served from our own
      domain. The site stores the following in your browser:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Theme preference</strong> (<code>localStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.theme</code>): saved only if you
        use the light/dark switch, so the site remembers your choice.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign-in session</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.session</code>): your session
        tokens after you sign in, kept only in that browser tab and removed when you close the tab or sign out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>App sign-in check</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.appSignIn</code>): a random
        check value while you sign in to the Mac or iPhone app from the browser, so the answer can only go back to that app; removed
        when sign-in finishes or the tab closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Google sign-in check</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.siteSignIn</code>): random
        check values while you sign in on this site with Google, so only your own sign-in is accepted when Google sends
        you back; removed as soon as you're back or the tab closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Invite code</strong> (<code>localStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.invite</code>): saved only if you
        open an invite link or enter an invite code, so it can be applied when you subscribe; removed after you subscribe
        or remove the code.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Chosen plan</strong> (<code>localStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.plan</code>): the plan (monthly or
        yearly) you picked on the pricing page, so it is still selected after you sign in, even in another tab or on a
        later visit; removed once you subscribe.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Continue to checkout</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.checkout</code>): a mark
        that you picked a plan on the pricing page and want to go on to payment, kept only in that browser tab until the
        payment page opens or the tab closes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tab identifier</strong> (<code>sessionStorage</code>, key <code>gistsmith.tab</code>): a random value that
        lets a duplicated tab notice it is a copy, so it asks you to sign in again instead of signing you out everywhere.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      These are strictly necessary for features you ask for, so they don't require consent. Third-party services load
      only on the pages that need them. On the <a href={routes.signIn}>sign-in page</a>, Apple's sign-in script is loaded
      from Apple, and signing in with Google takes you to Google's own sign-in page; Apple and Google may use their own
      cookies and process your IP address and browser information when you sign in, under their privacy policies. On the{" "}
      <a href={routes.contact}>contact page</a>, Cloudflare Turnstile checks that the form is used by a person; it
      processes technical data such as your IP address and browser characteristics. Cloudflare, which hosts the site,
      processes IP addresses to deliver and protect it.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Connecting an AI assistant.</strong> The sign-in and approval pages at mcp.gistsmith.com, which you see only
      when you connect an AI assistant (section 3), set one strictly necessary cookie that ties the connection request to
      your browser for 10 minutes. These pages load no statistics script and send no referrer.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Visitor statistics.</strong> To see how the site is used, we use Cloudflare Web Analytics, with Cloudflare as
      our processor. Its script sets no cookies, uses no local or session storage and doesn't fingerprint your browser or
      follow you across sites. We see only aggregated numbers: page views, referring sites, countries and device types.
      The script doesn't run on the shared-note page (gistsmith.com/s), whose address holds the note's key, or on your account page.
      Cloudflare doesn't use your IP address or browser details to identify you. Our legal basis is our legitimate interest in understanding and improving the website (GDPR Art. 6(1)(f)).
    </p>
  </LegalSection>

